    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on speed and favorable matchups to navigate the scarcity of our 5x5 categories. Our primary engine for production today is **Corbin Carroll**, whose 78.62 efficiency score makes him the anchor of our outfield. With a favorable matchup against Keller and a dynamic platoon advantage, **Corbin Carroll** provides the elite blend of power and stolen base upside we prioritize. Hot on his heels is **James Wood**, who sits at a 76.28 score; his move to the leadoff spot is a massive boon, and the combination of an elite 4.85 xERA opposing starter and his recent .378 xwOBA suggests he is primed to provide high-leverage counting stats. **Mickey Moniak** also demands attention at 75.98, benefiting immensely from an elite park factor and a high-order lineup slot against Scott.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved our bench, specifically regarding **Jose Caballero**. While his raw base projections remain strong, the optimizer docked him heavily due to his current status outside the confirmed starting lineup. Even with his elite 59% stolen base efficiency against the opposing pitcher, we are protecting our game caps by keeping him benched until his order position is solidified. Elsewhere, we are leaning on **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) to stabilize our RBI production against Cecconi, buoyed by favorable wind conditions. Conversely, we are exercising caution with **Trevor Story** (52.39); while his propensity for stolen bases against slow-delivering pitchers is noted, he faces a tough 3.02 xERA matchup against Jax, capping his potential ceiling today.\n\nWe are monitoring the status of **Luis Campusano** closely, as his sub-60 score (52.71) is contingent on an assumed #8 spot in the lineup. If **Luis Campusano** slides any lower or receives a rest day, we would be forced to pivot. We also remain optimistic that our inactive hitters, such as **Josh Naylor**, **Brice Turang**, and **Seiya Suzuki**, will return to active rotations soon to help us navigate the mid-week cap constraints. For now, we are banking on the high-ceiling output of **Tyler Soderstrom** and the steady glove-and-bat combo of **Kyle Karros** to maintain our efficiency across the board.",
